sible: G8.\n\nPlaying **G8** extends to four-in-a-row: G8-O-O-O-O from G8 to J8.\n\nBut wait — G8 to J8 would be four whites: G8, H8, I8, J8 — yes, if G8 is played.\n\nCurrently, only H8, I8, J8 are white. So G8 is empty. Playing G8 gives White a four-in-a-row threat.\n\nIs there a better move?\n\nWhat about creating a double threat?\n\nCheck diagonals or verticals involving existing white stones.\n\nH8: can it go upward diagonally? G7, F6, etc.? All empty.\n\nI8: same.\n\nJ8: down is J9 (X), so no vertical extension.\n\nNo other white stones elsewhere.\n\nSo the strongest immediate threat is extending the horizontal three to four by playing **G8**, creating a **straight four** (G8-J8), which forces Black to block at F8 next turn — but we don’t win immediately.\n\nBut is there a **winning move**?\n\nNo current five, nor any four-in-a-row.\n\nBut wait — what about **K9** or some fork?\n\nNo — White has no stones near those areas.\n\nAlternatively, could **I9** or **H10** form a diagonal?\n\nTry diagonal from H8: going down-right: I9, J10 — J10 is X, I9 is empty. But I9 would make H8-I9? That’s not aligned.\n\nDiagonals:\n- From G7 to J10: H8, I9, J10 — H8 is O, I9 is empty, J10 is X → cannot complete.\n- From F6 to I9: G7, H8, I9 — only H8 is O.\n\nNo viable diagonal.\n\nVertical? H8, H9 — H9 is empty, but no support.\n\nSo best move remains **G8**, extending the horizontal line to create a four-in-a-row threat.\n\nThis is a **forcing move**: after G8, White threatens to play F8 next for five, so Black must respond at F8.\n\nBut is G8 safe? Could Black have a counter-threat?\n\nBlack has:\n- K8, J9, J10 — three in a column? K8 is at (K,8), J9=(J,9), J10=(J,10) — different columns.\n\nJ8 is O, so J8, J9, J10: J8=O, J9=X, J10=X — so Black has J9 and J10 — only two in a row vertically.\n\nNo immediate Black win threat.\n\nSo White is free to build.\n\nTherefore, **G8** is the strongest move to build toward victory.\n\nAlso, G8 is not in the occupied list: Occupied = H8,I8,J8,K8,J9,J10,B11 → G8 is free.\n\nValid move.\n\nCould F8 be better? F8 is further away — playing F8 would make a disconnected two-in-a-row with H8? No — F8, then G8 empty — gap. Not good.\n\nSo G8 is adjacent and extends the chain.\n\nFinal evaluation: G8 is optimal.\n\nReason:  \nExtending the horizontal three-in-a-row (H8-I8-J8) to the left by playing G8 creates a strong four-in-a-row threat. This is a forcing move that pressures Black to respond, giving White initiative. No immediate win exists, but this maximizes threat potential in the most active zone.\n\nAnswer:  \n{"col": "G", "row": 8}'}
